@IlshatGaripov Grid Search and Euler-like Search are the ways to generate set of parameters for Lean Algorithm to be run with. For example, we have parameters a = [1..100], b=[10..1000] (can be as many as you need). That strategies generate pairs of parameters (a,b), i.e. (1, 10), (2, 10) etc. After that we run Lean process for each parameters set and get Lean statistics for each parameter set. As a result of optimization process you find the best values of your parameters (a = 16, b = 524).

Grid Search = iterating range with the fixed step size ([https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brute-force_search](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brute-force_search)). This method is good enough but it is not very accurate. On the one hand you need as small step as possible (to get accurate results), on the other hand each Lean run takes some time and is not very cheap in term of performance. That's why we also developed another strategy with Euler method idea in mind  (it's not exactly Euler method for solving differential equations) - instead of iterating all data points on the grid with small step, we iterate grid with big step, and then reduce it on the next iteration etc etc. 
 
As you noticed there can be implemented many different strategies (I didn't investigate Ore's grapth theory though), but we considered [genetic algorithms](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genetic_algorithm), [heuristic technique](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A*_search_algorithm), Monte-Carlo methods ([Simulated annealing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simulated_annealing)).  In the end we decided to come up with something simple and straightforward in first revision. I believe we will implement more different strategies in future. For this case provide an interface `IOptimisationStrategy` to abstract specific optimization strategy.
